The memorystatus kills of the jetsam configuration that a macOS userspace is not written for.

This kernel serves macOS with the board's embedded memory options (CONFIG_JETSAM), which Apple's macOS kernels
do not have. Two of the jetsam kills have no counterpart in Apple's macOS policy (memorystatus_pick_action
without CONFIG_JETSAM) and worked against the system on sheng (five boots of five to seven minutes each, counted
from the kernel log):
- Highwater kills, 196. With the available pages below the soft threshold (15/4 of 4 % of memory: 730 MB of the
  4864 MiB window) every process above its soft limit is killed. macOS sets soft limits as diagnostic marks: its
  kernel sends one EXC_RESOURCE report and lets the process run. The killed jobs are relaunched at once and grow
  past the mark again: iconservicesagent (450 MB) 62 times and
  com.apple.dock.extra (15 MB) 113 times, 158 of the kills with a full corpse for the crash reporter.
- Compressor thrashing kills, 5. They take the first process of the idle band, which under macOS is a clean
  daemon of about 1 MB (amfid twice, keybagd). Apple's arm64 macOS kernel runs no thrashing detection and swaps.
On XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX neither kill is taken and a soft limit crossing no longer marks a highwater candidate (the
flag would wake the jetsam thread at every page count update). Fatal limits, idle exits and the kills for page
shortage, compressor space and zone map exhaustion stay. iPadOS is not affected.
diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c b/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus.c
@@ -5163,12 +5163,19 @@
 			memorystatus_log_error("task_exceeded_footprint: failed to kill the current task (exiting?).\n");
 		}
 	} else {
+#if !XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
 		/*
 		 * HWM offender exists. Done without locks or synchronization.
 		 * See comment near its declaration for more details.
 		 */
 		os_atomic_store(&memorystatus_hwm_candidates, true, release);
 		_memstat_consider_waking_jetsam_thread();
+#endif /* !XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
+		/*
+		 * A macOS userspace has no highwater kill (memorystatus_pick_action), so a soft limit crossing
+		 * leaves no candidate: a set flag would wake the jetsam thread at every page count update below
+		 * the soft threshold with nothing for it to do.
+		 */
 
 #if VM_PRESSURE_EVENTS
 		/*
diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus_policy.c b/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus_policy.c
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus_policy.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_memorystatus_policy.c
@@ -88,7 +88,17 @@
 		&status->msh_available_pages_below_idle,
 		&status->msh_available_pages_below_critical,
 		&status->msh_available_pages_below_reaper);
+#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
+	/*
+	 * A macOS userspace on the jetsam configuration. Apple's arm64 macOS kernel runs no thrashing detection
+	 * (compressor_needs_to_swap) and its policy below has no thrashing kill: the compressor swaps instead. A
+	 * thrashing kill takes the first process of the idle band, which under macOS is a small clean daemon
+	 * (amfid, keybagd: about 1 MB each), so it relieves nothing. Compressor and swap exhaustion still kill.
+	 */
+	status->msh_compressor_is_thrashing = false;
+#else /* XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
 	status->msh_compressor_is_thrashing = !memorystatus_swap_all_apps && vm_compressor_is_thrashing();
+#endif /* XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
 #if CONFIG_PHANTOM_CACHE
 	status->msh_phantom_cache_pressure = os_atomic_load(&memorystatus_phantom_cache_pressure, relaxed);
 #else
@@ -361,10 +371,22 @@
 			return MEMORYSTATUS_KILL_TOP_PROCESS;
 		}
 
+#if XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
+		/*
+		 * A macOS userspace on the jetsam configuration. macOS sets soft (non-fatal) limits as diagnostic
+		 * marks: Apple's macOS kernel answers a crossing with one EXC_RESOURCE report and never kills for
+		 * it ("no early highwatermark killing" in the branch without CONFIG_JETSAM below). Killing here
+		 * restarts the same job, which grows past its mark again: iconservicesagent (soft limit 450 MB) and
+		 * com.apple.dock.extra (15 MB) were killed and relaunched every few seconds. Fatal limits still kill
+		 * at the crossing (memorystatus_on_ledger_footprint_exceeded).
+		 */
+		(void) highwater_remaining;
+#else /* XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
 		if (highwater_remaining) {
 			*kill_cause = kMemorystatusKilledHiwat;
 			return MEMORYSTATUS_KILL_HIWATER;
 		}
+#endif /* XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
 	}
 
